<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aten was the disc of the sun worshipped as a god in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, depicted as a sun whose rays end in human hands. Already honoured under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, it was raised by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> in the fourteenth century BCE above all other gods, whose cults, especially that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, were suppressed. After Akhenaten's death the traditional cults were restored and the Aten's temples were dismantled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aten.jpg" alt="Aten" width="320" height="139" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AtonX · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aten.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word aten appears in the Old Kingdom for anything flat and circular, and the sun was called the disc of the day, where Ra was thought to reside. The Aten was originally an aspect of Ra. It has no creation myth or family and is mentioned in the Book of the Dead. The first known reference to the sun disc as a deity is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-sinuhe">Story of Sinuhe</a> of the 12th Dynasty. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> the Aten was the pre-eminent creator deity of a pantheon and was shown as a falcon-headed god like Ra. Under his successor Amenhotep IV, who took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, it became the sole god of the state religion, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a> the Aten created all countries and peoples and cares for every creature. Its rays give life to the royal family, and others receive it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a> in exchange for loyalty, so the royal family stands as intermediary in worship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a> shows the god as creator and giver of life. Whether this was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> is debated: some scholars speak of quasi-monotheism, others of monolatry, since Akhenaten did not deny other gods but did not worship them, and others of henotheism. The god was shown only as a disc with rays ending in human hands, because it was held to be beyond creation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The cult centre was the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> founded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, today called Amarna. Its temples were open and unroofed to let in the sun, and the larger covered about 800 by 300 metres. There were no statues, which were seen as idolatry; Akhenaten and his family were shown venerating the Aten and receiving the ankh from it. Offerings of meat, wine and fruit were set on open-air altars, and the daily rite of purifying and clothing the divine image was not performed.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>, whose early name Tutankhaten meant &quot;living image of the Aten&quot;, reopened the state temples and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> as the pre-eminent solar deity. The Aten faded within about ten years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He raised the <b>Aten</b> above all other gods, suppressed the Amun cult, and moved the capital to Akhetaten.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1350 BCE the pharaoh Amenhotep IV broke with this tradition: renaming himself Akhenaten, he exalted the <b>Aten</b>, the visible disc of the sun, above all other gods, closed or neglected their temples, had the name of Amun hacked out of monuments and founded a new capital at…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Hymn to the Aten is the longest of several hymn-poems addressed to the <b>Aten</b>, the sun-disk worshipped as the supreme god under the 18th Dynasty pharaoh Akhenaten.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li></ul></section>
